WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.037
Welcome to Module 12, Views and Modular Queries.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:09.862
This module is about turning useful SQL logic into reusable database interfaces.

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:14.987
We will learn standard views, materialized views, updatable views, check options, dependency inspection,

00:00:14.987 --> 00:00:19.282
freshness policies, and modular query design.

00:00:19.282 --> 00:00:21.802
The central idea is that a view is not just saved text.

00:00:21.802 --> 00:00:27.472
It is an interface with a row grain, a set of columns,

00:00:27.472 --> 00:00:28.807
and a meaning that downstream users may depend on.

00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:35.282
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:41.202
an analytics team exposing a stable reporting view to dashboard users.

00:00:41.202 --> 00:00:45.982
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:49.492
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:49.492 --> 00:00:56.527
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:56.527 --> 00:01:00.087
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:00.087 --> 00:01:04.229
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
